633 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563633 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e733f8a2-e030-5a6d-ae1a-9d5ff92e104f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563638 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f54036d0-b672-540d-9df5-0a5f88f5edb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563647 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563648 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded points $t=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{t}$ is continuous for $t\\ge 0.$\n\n- The function $t+3$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for all $t\\ge 0$ except the point $t=1.$ So the interval of continuity is $[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563652 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+{lim}_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,j-5{t}^{2}\\,k$ is differentiable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563658 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{t})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=0,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{t-4})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(t-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $-5{t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{(-5{t}^{2})}^{′}=-10t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-5{t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ except the point $t=0,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is differentiable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563679 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one by one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{′}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is not differentiable at this points.\n\n- The derivative of  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ except the points $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{t-3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{2}^{t-2}\\,j+({t}^{2}-4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563682 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\frac{1}{t-3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$, as it  is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,3)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=3$ and the function is not defined for $t>3.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable on $(-4,4)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 4$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>4.$\n\n- The second component ${2}^{t-2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{2}-4$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-4,4).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cap (-4,4)=(-4,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[-1,1]?$\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(s) & =|{s}^{2}-1|\\,i+\\frac{s+1}{s}\\,j+2{s}^{2}\\,k \\\\ q(s) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)\\,i+({s}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,k \\\\ r(s) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,i-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,j+\\operatorname{csc}⁡s\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563683 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.  \n\nLet's consider the functions one by one. \n\n- The function $p(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits second component, $\\frac{s+1}{s},$ is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $q(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits first component, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)$, is undefined at $s=1$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $r(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits third component, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡s$, is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1].$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563690 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563691 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨t-1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,|t|,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563709 #edb/ref 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in [0,1].$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one by one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $[0,1],$ we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the third component of this vector, $\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}},$ is undefined at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that the function $q(t)$ is not differentiable on $[0,1].$\n\n- Finally, since the deviative of the second component $|t|$ of $r(t)$ is undefined at $t=0,$ we conclude that $r(t)$ is not differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"$p(t)$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563711 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one-by-one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded point $t=0.$\n\n- The function ${t}^{2}-1$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{t-2}$ is continuous everywhere except the point $t=2.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous everywhere except the points $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={t}^{2}+2 \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t-3}\\,i+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563737 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt[3]{t-3}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second and third components, ${cos}^{2}⁡t$ and $\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t\\sqrt{t},\\,1-{t}^{2},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563743 #edb/ref 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$ if and only if each of its component functions is differentiable on that interval. \n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$ to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we have that $g(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${h}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $h(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: \n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2{e}^{t-1},\\,\\sqrt{5-t}⟩$ is continuous." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563748 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $2{e}^{t-1}$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{5-t}$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,5].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j+(5+t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563757 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt[3]{t}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at  $t=0.$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{2t}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $(5+t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is \n\n$$\n[(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )]\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1{|}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563758 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1{|}^{3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)$ is differentiable over $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(1,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable over $(-2,2).$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}$ is differentiable for $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{3}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(1,2).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is\n\n$$\n(1,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)=(1,2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
